bear markets it's something we haven't  seen in US stocks and roughly a decade  depending on how you look at it they can  either be a major pain triggering panic  or for those with a strong stomach  lucrative buying opportunities either  way investors are on the hunt for the  next bear you'll spot one when the  market drops twenty percent or more from  its peak predicting the next one can be  really tough even for professionals but  there are some key charts that top  investors have found helpful at  predicting bear markets in the past so  let's take a look at how some of these  fared before the last two bear markets  first up the yield curve when the gap  between long term and short term  government bond yields is climbing like  this it generally means investors think  the economy is healthy and growth will  be stronger in the future but when it  starts to shrink and goes negative like  this that's when you might want to watch  out this is the dreaded inverted yield  curve and it usually means investors are  really scared about the future of the  economy in 2006-2007 it inverted as the  Fed lifted rates to try to cool the  housing market a bear market followed as  well as the worst financial crisis since  the Great Depression since 2014 the  yield curve has been flattening bringing  us once again pretty close to an  inverted curve so is it an ominous sign  no one's really sure there's a heated  debate some analysts say that years of  never before tried monetary policies  like negative interest rates in the  eurozone and Japan have distorted  long-term rates blurring the picture  here's an indicator about investor  sentiment zoomed out like this it kind  of looks like an electrocardiogram but  here's what you need to know when the  charts really high investors are feeling  optimistic and when it's really low  they're feeling pretty bearish let's  have a look at January 2000 the highest  point ever recorded on this survey the  world wide web was all the rage the  Nasdaq was going vertical and investors  would buy pretty much anything with  dot-com in its name I think we all know  how that one turned out this chart is  not always predictive but keep an eye on  the extremes when investors get really  really bullish it can sometimes be a  sign that their expectations are just  too high  and there's not a lot of cash left on  the sidelines to cushion any blows on  the flipside really low levels of  optimism actually have the most  predictive power let's take a look at  March 2009 sentiment on the survey was  at its lowest in years just before the  bear market ended if you bought the  market right at its bottom you'd be  sitting on a roughly three hundred  percent return today another sign of  excess deal activity companies snapping  up other companies just before the  dot-com bubble burst in 2000 you had a  flurry of merger activity including Time  Warner's deal with AOL just before the  financial crisis recession in bear  market deal activity spiked again RBS is  deal to buy ABN AMRO the biggest bank  merger ever marked the top so why do  mergers matter they can signal that  companies have gotten excessively  optimistic or that they actually think  deals are the only way to grow as the  economy slows down  lastly jobless claims major bear markets  usually but don't always coincide with  recessions here in both 2000 and 2007  jobless claims start to climb right at  the beginning of what turned out to be  major bear markets steady rise in claims  can signal that something is starting to  go wrong with the labor market when the  job market slows consumers tend to spend  less hitting profits of course none of  these charts on their own can really  give you a concrete answer as to when  the next bear is going to emerge but  taken together they can give you a  pretty useful indicator about the health  of the market so when do you think the  next bear market is due take our poll on  wsj.com or tweet me at gold Reba
 

